1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,300
Okay, now if you guys are visual workers like I am, you kind of need to see some things.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:47,060
I am going to give you kind of the overall snapshot. I told you we're going to jump into Merrill here. This is a board that I've used to basically create and ideate workflows for beginning to end. You can even see some of these here that I have handwritten on a whiteboard previously.

3
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:54,200
But overall, the first thing that we're going to do is take this essentially as much as we can step by step.

4
00:00:54,200 --> 00:01:24,200


5
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:37,420
People think they know about the automation that they want to do. They get into it. They start mapping it all out. And then once they create it, they realize that it solves nothing. Don't do that. I've done it before. It's just a complete waste of time.

6
00:01:37,420 --> 00:02:07,420


7
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:37,420


8
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:45,160
You want to keep the subject matter expert out of it, the business owner out of it. You want to keep the person that knows the most in the loop for the longest time possible so you get the best results.

9
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:58,060
Now, that being said, this is also where you're going to just jot down all of the tools that you use. These are most of the tools that I use. Get everything out as much as you can.

10
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:06,960
I would like to actually reduce this. The more that you do reduce this, you reduce your overhead, your subscriptions. You also reduce information islands and data silos.

11
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:14,840
We'll talk about that. And if we haven't gone over that yet or you haven't seen any of my other videos, I do have one that's specifically about data silos.

12
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:24,360
Because the other thing that you have to think about, the more you have, the more tools you have, the more information islands or data silos you're inevitably going to have.

13
00:03:24,360 --> 00:03:33,800
So each and every department, whether it's marketing, admin, sales, rev ops, sec ops, whatever it is, everybody uses their own set of tools.

14
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:41,780
So that creates all of these data silos. So the more that you have and the larger of a business that you have, the more tools you're going to have, the more data silos you're going to have.

15
00:03:42,020 --> 00:03:42,900
But that's a whole different topic.

16
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:52,160
Now, one of the reasons why you want to get this out of your brain is because essentially, like I brought up earlier, you might automate the wrong thing.

17
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:55,260
Like this happens quite often.

18
00:03:56,040 --> 00:04:03,320
Maybe you have a meeting with your team and you guys are going over the concept of it or somebody missed that day or whatever it is.

19
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:12,320
Having it out on a whiteboard or in notes keeps everybody on the same page of what you need to automate and why.

20
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:21,220
There are many businesses that jump into automation and they're trying to reduce bottlenecks and be the most impactful that they can.

21
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:26,140
But they somehow missed one of the steps or are completely automating the wrong thing.

22
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:30,580
And mapping forces you to see where value leaks are.

23
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:34,500
So you don't waste time building automations that save minutes instead of hours.

24
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:42,160
Sometimes just a slight pivot in an automation, instead of only saving five minutes, it saves two hours.

25
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:48,540
So once you actually put it all on paper, get it on a whiteboard, it'll give you a lot more clarity.

26
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:52,600
Which moves on to step two, hidden dependencies.

27
00:04:54,540 --> 00:04:56,820
A workflow isn't just a set of tasks.

28
00:04:57,080 --> 00:05:05,920
There are things sometimes that have dependencies before it or that need to be worked out in order for the workflow to happen.

29
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:08,180
Maybe it's a meeting.

30
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:12,780
Maybe it is a tool that you have yet to use or pay for the subscription for.

31
00:05:12,940 --> 00:05:20,660
Or in some cases, like we talked about earlier, you want to integrate it into a piece of software that doesn't have API integration.

32
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:22,240
So you have to rethink that.

33
00:05:22,360 --> 00:05:25,180
There are hidden dependencies that are out there all the time.

34
00:05:25,180 --> 00:05:32,800
And then without mapping, you don't see step two secretly depends on step zero or step one.

35
00:05:33,180 --> 00:05:38,720
Like uploading the right file format or client signing a document or anything along those lines.

36
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:46,440
Or you're trying to automate something that requires credentials and prior verification like OAuth or passwords.

37
00:05:46,440 --> 00:05:58,420
Or you are trying to automate your Stripe invoices and you don't realize you have to convert from one file format to another before you can actually upload.

38
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:01,960
So you have to think of these dependencies.

39
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:06,980
They're not always going to be just bam, smack you in the face.

40
00:06:07,140 --> 00:06:10,120
It's just better to try to get it out on the front end.

41
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:14,440
Next is garbage in, garbage out.

42
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:18,320
But AI plus automation only amplifies what's already there.

43
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:22,200
For a really simple example, ChatGPT.

44
00:06:22,580 --> 00:06:29,620
If you say, hey, ChatGPT, create a personalized email that I want to send to this new prospect.

45
00:06:29,760 --> 00:06:30,500
It's the first time.

46
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:32,740
Give me that draft.

47
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:38,780
That's completely different than saying, hey, this is the customer profile that I'm going after.

48
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:42,380
It's the first time we have had zero in action.

49
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:43,240
This is their company.

50
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:46,700
This is a value proposition.

51
00:06:46,920 --> 00:06:54,120
And you actually build that prompt out instead of saying just send out a personalized email to Chris Hawkins.

52
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:55,360
You can actually have a company.

53
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:56,600
You can have enriched data.

54
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:58,180
And you can give it over to ChatGPT.

55
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:00,960
So better in is better out.

56
00:07:01,220 --> 00:07:03,840
In other words, garbage in, garbage out.

57
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:08,120
The worse you either explain or you leave out details.

58
00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:13,480
ChatGPT, as well as AI and AI agents, they don't know anything that you don't tell them.

59
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:15,940
So garbage in is always going to be garbage out.

60
00:07:16,880 --> 00:07:21,320
Mapping exposes where your data is created, validated, and passed along.

61
00:07:21,320 --> 00:07:24,620
So again, garbage in, garbage out.

62
00:07:24,940 --> 00:07:26,920
So education, a lot of people don't think about.

63
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:32,100
What I just kind of mentioned before, humans intuitively, they handle exceptions here.

64
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:43,940
Meaning, like, say we are, I don't know, sending an invoice to somebody that actually hasn't paid yet.

65
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:50,920
And we know, oh, okay, looking at our calendar, I'm working with Jim Roof and Company.

66
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:53,200
And they're actually on a project.

67
00:07:53,660 --> 00:07:55,620
And I know the hours that they work.

68
00:07:56,220 --> 00:07:58,240
And now they're on vacation.

69
00:07:58,380 --> 00:08:00,040
I see, oh, we talked.

70
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:00,760
We had a meeting.

71
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:01,780
We're going to reconvene.

72
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:03,420
We've got another project that's coming up.

73
00:08:03,620 --> 00:08:05,100
They haven't paid their invoice yet.

74
00:08:05,780 --> 00:08:06,800
Humans can do that.

75
00:08:07,860 --> 00:08:08,660
Machines don't.

76
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:14,960
They're not going to know Jim is on vacation or they're out of town or what it is.

77
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:18,700
They're not going to intuit anything that isn't there.

78
00:08:19,060 --> 00:08:21,880
Only what is available to them will they be able to process.

79
00:08:23,060 --> 00:08:28,280
Mapping lets you identify the edge cases up front and decide whether you automate, delegate, or eliminate.

80
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:33,800
And the next one, clarity for compliance and trust.

81
00:08:34,140 --> 00:08:36,140
This works in a multitude of ways.

82
00:08:36,220 --> 00:08:37,040
We talked about HIPAA.

83
00:08:37,040 --> 00:08:39,440
We talked about very risk-averse lawyers.

84
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:48,880
Or maybe there's PCI compliance, payment card information, payment card industry, things that you have to actually be aware of.

85
00:08:49,720 --> 00:08:59,760
Mapping it out provides clarity and compliance and trust, especially in the regulated spaces, like I just said, and PCI compliance and HIPAA and all those things.

86
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:03,340
And then six, it forces the process of discipline.

87
00:09:03,340 --> 00:09:06,740
Most businesses run on tribal knowledge.

88
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:09,460
Like, oh, Jenna knows how to do that.

89
00:09:09,540 --> 00:09:10,700
We're just going to go give it to Jenna.

90
00:09:11,020 --> 00:09:15,060
And nobody really knows what Jenna does because she hasn't mapped it all out.

91
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:16,640
There's no standard operating procedure.

92
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:18,180
So that is a bottleneck.

93
00:09:18,480 --> 00:09:19,600
Oh, I don't know how to do it.

94
00:09:19,780 --> 00:09:20,200
Jenna does.

95
00:09:20,380 --> 00:09:21,740
I'm just going to pass it over to her.

96
00:09:21,740 --> 00:09:32,240
So it forces discipline to where you have to get this out of your brain or out of Jenna's brain and put it onto a particular document that you can create SOPs around.

97
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:41,400
You start to discover opportunities and things to eliminate before or even after automation.

98
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:44,160
Not every step deserves automation.

99
00:09:44,420 --> 00:09:51,940
Some things that you do still want to do manually because they're very high value situations.

100
00:09:52,220 --> 00:09:56,280
Sometimes I've had situations where people are selling very, very high ticket offers.

101
00:09:56,280 --> 00:10:02,760
You don't want to automate a lot of those cold outreaches or those sales calls.

102
00:10:02,980 --> 00:10:04,500
Like, you do have retail AI.

103
00:10:04,720 --> 00:10:06,040
Voice agents are getting great.

104
00:10:06,140 --> 00:10:07,300
They sound just like humans.

105
00:10:07,440 --> 00:10:11,360
Their knowledge base is deep because everything that you can train on is awesome.

106
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:16,860
But you don't want an AI cold calling a potential $50,000 contract.

107
00:10:17,380 --> 00:10:19,100
Like, that's just not something you do.

108
00:10:19,220 --> 00:10:21,260
So not everything needs to be automated.

109
00:10:21,260 --> 00:10:26,560
You can automate everything all the way up into that call, but you don't want to automate that.

110
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:27,400
And some people are.

111
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:27,980
I can tell you.

112
00:10:28,060 --> 00:10:36,140
There are people out there that are using retail AI and a couple of other things, Vappy or whatever, and they're trying to do high ticket closes.

113
00:10:36,260 --> 00:10:37,100
Some of them are successful.

114
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:38,420
I wouldn't trust it.

115
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:43,740
But this is where you start to discover those opportunities or eliminate what you need to be automated.

116
00:10:43,740 --> 00:10:55,820
And then mapping often shows, like, redundant tools or overlap or whatever, like Notion plus Google Docs or, you know, using multiple Excel sheets or whatever it is.

117
00:10:56,360 --> 00:11:01,780
The bottom line, automation without mapping is like paving a cow path.

118
00:11:01,780 --> 00:11:14,200
If you just automation without mapping is just something that you don't want to do.

119
00:11:14,300 --> 00:11:15,300
Mapping gives you the clarity.

120
00:11:16,260 --> 00:11:18,380
It surfaces all of the edge cases.

121
00:11:18,680 --> 00:11:23,740
It allows you to look at all of the dependencies and just creates an overall cleaner flow.

122
00:11:25,560 --> 00:11:28,960
Now, that being said, that's kind of like the structure of how you want to do it.

123
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:37,700
We'll go over quickly just a few things just so that you have some kind of material examples of getting your beak wet into N8N.

124
00:11:37,820 --> 00:11:42,740
These are little screen grabs of little nodes that are from N8N.

125
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:47,080
So you have entry points, you have functions, and you have exit points.

126
00:11:47,820 --> 00:11:49,280
Entry points are things like triggers.

127
00:11:49,540 --> 00:11:55,920
Maybe it is a weekly trigger or a daily trigger or you just got an email and that starts the workflow.

128
00:11:55,920 --> 00:12:00,880
A trigger is something that actually starts the first part of the automation.

129
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:02,800
So that is the input.

130
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:05,360
And then there are all of the functions.

131
00:12:05,580 --> 00:12:09,620
Code nodes, custom nodes, edit fields, splitting out fields, filters.

132
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:13,460
You have AI agents.

133
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:18,940
Maybe those AI agents have access to your calendar or other tools like Perplexity or Google.

134
00:12:18,940 --> 00:12:21,720
Google, your Gmail and different things like that.

135
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:22,860
Those are your functions.

136
00:12:23,080 --> 00:12:25,660
So there's the input.

137
00:12:26,100 --> 00:12:30,680
There are all of the functions, the meat and potatoes, the executions of what's happening with your AI.

138
00:12:31,040 --> 00:12:33,520
And then there is the exit, the out points.

139
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:43,240
Those are things like updating spreadsheets or putting files into your Dropbox or to Airtable or to ClickUp or to your HubSpot or different things like that.

140
00:12:43,240 --> 00:12:49,780
So that's a very rudimentary way to kind of go over input, function, output.

141
00:12:50,240 --> 00:12:57,680
And these are some of the nodes that you're going to start to see and maybe you've worked with and it in already and you're kind of familiar with a few of these things.

142
00:12:57,680 --> 00:13:05,020
But overall, this is what something as a workflow starts to look like.

143
00:13:05,240 --> 00:13:07,320
You are listening for and collecting data.

144
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:10,320
So like an RSS feed to an Airtable.

145
00:13:10,700 --> 00:13:18,680
There is an RSS feed that you're either listening to, it filters out and executes a sub-workflow and then it inputs it over into Airtable.

146
00:13:20,380 --> 00:13:23,600
For Zoom meetings, this is what I used to use for Zapier.

147
00:13:23,720 --> 00:13:24,580
I use a little bit different.

148
00:13:24,580 --> 00:13:33,160
This is a very old board, but in Zapier, you would have something like ReadAI, format it with date and time and then other formats.

149
00:13:33,260 --> 00:13:37,640
And then you inject it into Airtable or HubSpot or Salesforce or whatever it is.

150
00:13:39,740 --> 00:13:42,780
Another one, something like this, like LinkedIn content analytics.

151
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:53,340
You have a schedule, you create a task in ClickUp, you send a message in Slack to whoever it is, maybe a channel or a specific team member or an overall team.

152
00:13:53,340 --> 00:13:54,700
You wait.

153
00:13:55,700 --> 00:13:56,420
Human in a loop.

154
00:13:56,880 --> 00:14:00,920
So the response from whoever it is, maybe it's like the sales director.

155
00:14:01,080 --> 00:14:03,940
You're saying, hey, we've got this task.

156
00:14:04,580 --> 00:14:06,140
This person needs to be onboarded.

157
00:14:06,540 --> 00:14:07,940
Have they already been onboarded?

158
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:08,480
Yes or no?

159
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:11,600
This little wait allows them to give a response.

160
00:14:11,700 --> 00:14:16,440
If no, then it'll get tasked and then it'll split it out for whatever operations that need to happen.

161
00:14:16,440 --> 00:14:20,360
Put it in an Airtable and send message and contact fields.

162
00:14:20,420 --> 00:14:24,100
And this is where you start to see a little bit of how things can work for you.

163
00:14:24,400 --> 00:14:27,100
Say you have like 15 of these that have stacked up.

164
00:14:27,260 --> 00:14:30,340
This little part right here, when you see this, it'll go through the loop.

165
00:14:30,340 --> 00:14:32,960
So it'll send it in one by one.

166
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:33,500
It'll wait.

167
00:14:33,740 --> 00:14:39,720
And then this, again, like maybe the sales director or sales manager has it and they've got to work through all 15.

168
00:14:40,040 --> 00:14:43,660
Every time they either approve it, yes or no, it'll go through this filtration system.

169
00:14:44,120 --> 00:14:46,820
If there's no operation, you know, they'll just do whatever there is.

170
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:52,340
If there is, then it'll execute whatever it is based on the if command that's right here in this.

171
00:14:52,340 --> 00:14:56,880
And then send a message through to Slack.

172
00:14:57,600 --> 00:15:00,220
And then it'll go again for another answer, human in the loop.

173
00:15:00,340 --> 00:15:06,000
And then the cycle either continues with more questions or it will go and execute whatever it is.

